      title: 'JUKHA',
      subtitle: 'Card game',
    },
    sections: [
      {
        type: 'about',
        tabLabel: 'About',
        pages: [
          {
            title: 'Jukha',
            subtitle: 'Card game',
            content: [
              {
                type: 'paragraph',
                text: "Jukha is a high-speed Israeli-Bulgarian 'hot potato' game where players compete to empty their hands by matching cards while avoiding (or passing) the unique Joker. It is a game of reflexes and nerve, as the Joker acts as a barrier to victory. It is almost identical in mechanics to the Bulgarian 'Joker' game but with specific variations in the 'Final Duel'.",
              },
              {
                type: 'paragraph',
                text: "Rooted in the Sephardic and Mizrahi communities in Israel who brought 'passing' games from the Balkans and Turkey. The name 'Jukha' (or 'Juxa') is a common moniker for the Joker card in these regions.",
              },
            ],
          },
          {
            title: 'Setup',
            subtitle: 'Table, deck, equipment, and deal',
            content: [
              {
                type: 'paragraph',
                text: 'Optimal with 4-6 players.',
              },
              {
                type: 'paragraph',
                text: 'Standard 52-card deck + 1 Joker.',
              },
              {
                type: 'paragraph',
                text: 'Standard 52-card deck + 1 Joker.',
              },
              {
                type: 'paragraph',
                text: 'Dealer shuffles and deals all cards out. If players have unequal counts, it is accepted as part of the game.',
              },
            ],
          },
          {
            title: 'Variations',
            subtitle: 'Known ways this game changes',
            content: [
              {
                type: 'list',
                style: 'unordered',
                items: [
                  {
                    text: 'Single loser: As soon as any player exits, play ends and the player holding the joker is the loser (may have been the original rule).',
                  },
                  {
                    text: 'Last two lose: When only two players remain, the player holding the joker is the loser.',
                  },
                  {
                    text: 'Double out: When only two players remain they compete in a final duel; both are losers unless one exits (regional variant).',
                  },
                ],
              },
            ],
          },
        ],
      },
    ],
